# Valgrind suppressions for hipObject's tests.
#
# Use this file with valgrind's --suppressions option. With CTest, configure
# with -DMEMORYCHECK_SUPPRESSIONS_FILE=<path to this file> and then run
# "ctest -T memcheck".
#
# Every entry here suppresses only "still reachable" blocks. Valgrind doesn't
# report those unless it's run with --show-leak-kinds=reachable or
# --show-leak-kinds=all. Leaks that are definitely, indirectly, or possibly
# lost are never suppressed.
#
# Each entry covers memory that a library allocates once and keeps until the
# process exits. Most entries match only allocations made under the function
# that does that one-time setup, so the same allocations made anywhere else
# are still reported. Those stacks can be deeper than valgrind's default of 12
# frames, and a stack that's cut off before the function doesn't match, so
# run valgrind with --num-callers=40 or more.
#
# Valgrind can't run optimized Clang builds of hipObject correctly, so use a
# GCC build or a Clang Debug build. hipObject builds with
# -ftrivial-auto-var-init=pattern when it's compiled with Clang. With that
# flag, Clang at -O1 and above copies std::format()'s arguments with the x87
# instructions fldt and fstpt. Valgrind emulates the x87 registers with 64
# bits instead of 80, so the copy loses bits: formatted numbers come out
# wrong, and formatting a string can crash. No suppression can fix that.

# The HIP runtime loads ROCm's LLVM and clang libraries. When those libraries
# load, their static constructors allocate memory that they never free.
# hipObject never calls LLVM directly; LLVM is loaded only as part of the HIP
# runtime. So any reachable block that LLVM allocated belongs to ROCm.
{
   rocm-libLLVM-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libLLVM.so*
}

{
   rocm-libclang-cpp-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libclang-cpp.so*
}

# hipObject does call the HIP runtime, so the next two entries only cover
# the runtime's one-time setup. A reachable block that HIP allocates at any
# other time, during a call from hipObject, could mean that hipObject didn't
# release something, so it is still reported.

# Memory the HIP runtime allocates while the dynamic loader (ld.so) is
# loading it
{
   rocm-libamdhip64-load-time-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libamdhip64.so*
   ...
   obj:*/ld-linux*.so*
}

# The first HIP call initializes the runtime once, with pthread_once(). This
# includes the HSA runtime's setup, in hsa_init().
{
   rocm-libamdhip64-first-call-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libamdhip64.so*
   fun:*pthread_once*
}

# systemPlatform() builds the platform that hipObjInit() uses the first time
# it's called, and deliberately never destroys it (see src/state.cpp). The
# platform keeps libibverbs loaded with dlopen(), so the platform's objects
# and the dynamic loader's records of libibverbs and its dependencies stay
# allocated until the process exits.
{
   hipobj-system-platform-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:*systemPlatform*
}

# OpenSSL, which libcurl uses, keeps some of its global state after
# curl_global_cleanup(), until the process exits.
{
   openssl-global-init-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:OPENSSL_init_crypto
}
